So I proceeded as discussed in the above posts and fit models both with year and with no covariates for availability and a variety of covariates for p, and in all cases availability was essentially one. Several possible NB models that had high support had NANs for availability or its SE, I'm guessing because its appears to be essentially 1.  So for comparison I stacked the three years of site visits and set Primary = 1 and refit models.  The density estimate (back transformed) and its SE for the stacked data was slightly greater  0.355 (0.024) versus .0,272 (0.017).  I was initially avoiding stacking the data because of pseudoreplication issues and concerns it would bias the SE, but this doesn't seem to be an big issue (I'm assuming the SE is actually larger because the abundance is larger).  So my questions are:
1) If I retain the multi year approach can I ignore errors for NANs for availability in some models if its essentially one if the model is other highly supported?
2)  Why should the abundance estimate be that much larger for the staked data?
3)  Should I just go ahead and stack the data if availability is essentially 1 and staking doesn't seem to greatly bias the SEs?
Thanks---Frank

