Thanks so much for your prompt reply, Andy. I can see where a data set having no variation in occupancy state can cause estimation problems (I have some familiarity with boundary issues from previous experience with mark-recapture models) and in this case it manifested in the SE of occupancy being inestimable (0.00). As you guessed, I was actually interested in estimating detection probability to compare the efficacy of different sampling methods if occupancy were the latent parameter of interest.

Because I do have count data, I was actually most interested in having the question answered from the perspective of estimating abundance. My understanding from reading the n-mixture literature is that it is not necessary to have unoccupied sites in the data set in order for the modeling to 'work', just that there is variation in the resulting counts acquired during the replicate site visits. Can you clarify that this is indeed correct?

Best,
Kiel

